# 088 — `wm`: window manager for text and pixel programs — done

**Type:** tools (application, `mind::tui` and `gfx::Screen` backends, launch grants) · **Owner:** tools track · **Priority:** P2 · **Status:** done (2026-10-05) · **Blocked by:** — (157 window broker and 156 PS/2 mouse are done) · **Roadmap:** track G · **Constitution:** MC-2.6, MC-2.11, MC-3.3, MC-3.11

## Problem

Every program has a whole screen of its own, and only the focused one is shown. There is no way to see the clock, the file manager and `top` side by side, or to move and resize what is shown.

## Plan

- **`wm` is an application, not a service.**
  - The shell starts it like `fm`, and it can run in any virtual console (155).
  - It holds only what the shell lends it and passes on only part of that (MC-3.11).
  - It is the *presentation* of windows. The windows themselves are kept by the window broker (157), so programs outlive `wm`. A graphical window manager later is another client of the same broker and protocol.
  - The window protocol (`idl/window.wit` and `mind::window`) is independent of `wm`. Programs do not depend on which manager shows them.
- **Desktop.** `wm` draws on its own screen. Windows sit on the cell grid (8×16): text frames, a title, a close mark and a resize corner, with z-order and keyboard focus. Two kinds of window content:
  - **text:** a cell grid the program draws with `mind::tui`;
  - **pixels:** a buffer of the window's size in the screen's pixel format, which the program draws with `gfx::Screen`.
  
  `wm` copies only the changed rectangle, which the surface header names with a change counter. A full 1280×800 screen is about 4 MiB a frame.
- **Moving and snapping:**
  - Move and resize with the keyboard (a move/resize mode with the arrows) and, with 156, by dragging the title or the corner.
  - A window brought within two cells of a screen edge sticks to it. At a corner it takes that quarter, at a side half the screen, at the top the whole screen.
  - Keys: Alt+arrows for halves, Alt+1…4 for quarters, Alt+Enter maximize/restore.
  - Every change of geometry is saved in the broker.
- **Programs in windows:**
  - A program started in window mode (`REQUEST_WINDOW`) gets a client of the broker in a fixed application slot. It creates its own surface and input endpoint and registers them (157).
  - The backends of `mind::tui::Terminal` and `gfx::Screen` draw into the surface, and `mind::input` reads the window's endpoint. So `fm`, `top`/`memmap`/`load`/`hw`, `view`, `edit` and `clock` run in a window unchanged.
  - A resize tells the program its new size.
  - `wm` forwards keys (and mouse events) only to the focused window's program. A program sees no other window's surface or keys.
  - `wm` starts programs through its own launch session, with only what it holds and the program asks for. It asks the shell for those grants when it starts, for example `REQUEST_FILES` to run `fm` with the user's files.
- **Stopping `wm`:** two ways, the same in a later graphical manager (157):
  - **detach** (the default way out, and what a crash does): `wm` ends, its programs keep running hidden, the broker keeps the layout, and the next `wm` shows the same windows where they were.
  - **close all:** every program gets a close event. Programs that do not end are listed, and `wm` stops them through a lifecycle client only if it holds one.
  
  Closing one window sends its program the close event. A program that ends loses its window.
- **What 157 and 156 already provide:**
  - **The broker:** service `windows`, `idl/window.wit`, `mind::window::Surface`. The surface is the broker's memory, lent to the program and the manager.
  - **Slot and flags:** `SLOT_WINDOW` 8 with `REQUEST_WINDOW` and `REQUEST_WINDOW_MANAGER`, lent by the shell and accepted by the loader.
  - **The mouse:** `mind::input::pointer`, `read_input`, `Input::Pointer`.
  - **Examples:** `wintest` and `winmgr` show both sides of the protocol.
  
  What remains here:
  - `wm` itself;
  - the surface backends of `mind::tui::Terminal` and `gfx::Screen`, plus reading input from `Surface::event` in `mind::input` for programs started with `REQUEST_WINDOW`;
  - `wm` lending its broker `client` to the programs it starts;
  - `clock` and the monitors declaring `REQUEST_WINDOW` (or a `--window` start).

## Acceptance criteria

QEMU suite (keys over the UART and `sendkey`, the mouse with `mouse_move`/`mouse_button` once 156 is done):
- `wm` opens `top`, `fm` and `clock` in three windows. A `screendump` shows the text frames, the text content and the clock's pixels.
- Moving a window to the left edge snaps it to the left half, to a corner a quarter. With 156, dragging a title moves and snaps it.
- Keys go only to the focused window.
- Detach: after `wm` ends (and after it is killed), `top` and `clock` still run. A new `wm` shows them at the same places.
- Close all: the programs end (`ps`).
- A program in a window gets no capability `wm` does not hold.
- Host tests:
  - window geometry (move, resize, snap, z-order);
  - the text and pixel surface backends render into a grid or buffer;
  - the changed-rectangle copy.

## Related

[157](157-window-broker.done), [089](089-text-clock-faces.done) (text faces of `clock` and `dzen-clock`), [156](156-ps2-mouse.done), [155](155-virtual-consoles.done) (full-screen consoles with Alt+F1…F4, which complement windows), [063](063-file-manager-read-only.done), [054](054-tui-library.done), [docs/tools](../docs/tools/README.md).

## Resolution

Asked for by the user: two `fm` windows, a clock and the Dzen clock side by side under one manager.

- **`wm`** (`wm/`: `src/desk.rs` the desktop, `src/main.rs` the broker client), an application with `REQUEST_WINDOW_MANAGER | REQUEST_FILES | REQUEST_SYSINFO`:
  - attaches with the manager client, adopts the broker's windows at their saved places (z-order too) and follows its generation every 250 ms: new windows are mapped (manager lease, wake endpoint), gone ones dropped;
  - draws on its own screen through `mind::tui::Terminal`: a top bar, the desktop, frames with title, `[×]` and `◆`, text content cell by cell, a status line; pixel windows are copied over the cells that show them (an owner map per cell, so overlapping windows clip them); the mouse pointer is drawn last;
  - `wm fm fm clock dzen-clock` or `wm fm data, edit ram:a.txt` starts programs; Alt+R starts more; keys and the mouse as in docs/tools §4.9 (Alt+Tab, Alt+arrows, Alt+1…4, Alt+Enter, Alt+M with snapping, Alt+W/Alt+F4, Alt+H, Alt+Q, Alt+X; dragging titles and corners);
  - keys go to the focused window's surface queue with a wake message; Shift/Ctrl/Alt are given to the window in front as held and to the others as released (a window that lost the focus in the middle of Alt+Tab kept Alt in its key bar before);
  - text windows are asked for the size of the frame's inside; every change of place and z-order is saved in the broker;
  - Alt+Q detaches; a killed or crashed `wm` is detached by the broker; the next `wm` shows the windows at their places. Alt+X closes all, waits up to 3 s and names programs still running.
  - Programs it starts get a plain broker client in `SLOT_WINDOW` and, of what they ask for, only what `wm` holds: the user's files (`REQUEST_FILES`), a file's directory confined from them (`REQUEST_FILE`), system information. The log says what was lent and what was not (`STARTED top PID n WITH window,sysinfo WITHOUT lifecycle`). Console programs are refused (they stay in the shell).
- **Programs in windows** (`libmind/src/windowed.rs`): with a broker client in `SLOT_WINDOW`, `Terminal::open(info, title)` opens a text window with room for the screen's cells (it starts at 80 × 25 and follows the size the manager asks for), and `mind::windowed::pixels(info, w, h, title)` returns a `BootInfo` whose framebuffer is a pixel window. `mind::input` reads the surface's event queue, `mind::time::sleep` waits on the window's wake endpoint (in 50 ms slices, so a missed wake costs little) and publishes pixel drawing, `wait_key_or_modifiers` returns when the size changes, and a closed window ends the program. `Terminal` writes changed cells into the surface with the changed rectangle and the cursor (`O_CURSOR` in the header), and `set_title`.
  - `fm`, `edit`, `view`, `keys` and the monitors (`top`, `memmap`, `load`, `hw`, `ipc`, `caps`) use `Terminal::open`; `fm` puts its active directory in the title; `clock` (320 × 176) and `dzen-clock` (400 × 320) use `pixels` — one line each. Started from the shell they run on their screen as before.
- **loader:** a program whose launch session lends `SLOT_WINDOW` and that is not a window manager gets no screen (a frame of kernel memory saved per window).
- **windows (157):** the manager's lease of a window whose program ended stays until the manager lists the windows again (it may be drawing it; memory revoked under it faulted `wm`); the program's lease and wake endpoint go at once. Also: `Surface::{size, fits, set_cursor, cursor, pixel, queued}`, `Key::letter` (Alt+R in either layout).
- **Tests:**
  - host: `tests/wm_host.rs` — placement of new windows, focus and z-order, halves, quarters, maximize, move/resize limits, snapping, hit testing, the keys wm keeps and passes on (also in the Russian layout), mouse drags, what each cell shows (text content, frames, pixel windows below others), every size; `tests/window_host.rs` — cursor, resize within the memory, pixels;
  - QEMU `wm` suite: `wm fm, clock, top` — the screen shows the frames, fm's and top's text and the clock's green digits; `cd docs` reaches fm and nothing reaches top; Alt+Right, Alt+3, Alt+Enter twice; Alt+M moves off the edge and Enter snaps to the left half; the clock's title dragged with the mouse snaps to the left half; `caps` started with Alt+R runs without the authority view and shows "No access to sysmon", Alt+W ends it; Alt+Q leaves with the programs running and a new `wm` restores the places, as after `kill`; Alt+X ends every program; the kernel heap is back where it was.
- **Fixed after the first version (reported by the user):**
  - a dialog of wm (Alt+H, Alt+R) over a pixel window was painted over by the window's pixels: a cell shows a window's pixels only while it still holds the pixel placeholder after everything is drawn (`desk::PIXELS`; host test in `what_each_cell_shows`);
  - wm took Alt+F1 for its help, so fm's Alt+F1 (the left panel's volume) opened wm's keys: Alt+H alone does it now, and the Alt keys fm and edit use reach them (host test in `keys_wm_keeps_and_passes_on`).
- **Not done:** text faces of `clock` and `dzen-clock` (089); pixel windows keep their size (the frame clips or pads); the mouse inside windows (pointer events are not forwarded); console programs in windows; a program does not learn it is hidden beyond the state field.
